{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"The US birth denominator for NIPT is shrinking faster in 2026 than the 2025 headline shows: the 12-month-ending general fertility rate fell from 54.5 (Q1'25) to 53.0 (Q1'26) and from 53.7 (Q2'25) to 52.7 (Q2'26).","detail":"These are NCHS VSRR 12-month-ending GFR figures per 1,000 females aged 15-44. The y/y drop is -2.8% in Q1'26 and -1.9% in Q2'26. Sequential steps were 54.5→54.6 (Q4'24)→53.3 (Q4'25)→53.0 (Q1'26)→52.7 (Q2'26), so the decline is ongoing, not a one-off. Scaling the confirmed 3,606,400 births in 2025 by 52.7/53.1 gives roughly 3.58M, or about 3.50-3.55M if the female 15-44 population is flat to slightly down. That is my estimate: the dataset holds rates only, not counts, and it assumes a constant population.","url":"https://data.cdc.gov/resource/76vv-a7x8.json","date":"2026 Q2 data (accessed 2026-10-12)","source_name":"CDC NCHS VSRR quarterly provisional estimates (dataset 76vv-a7x8)","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"strong","why_new":"Known items cover the 2025 births total and Taiwan births. This adds the US 12-month-ending rate through Q2'26 with y/y math."},
