{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"Births to older mothers, the cohort with the highest NIPT intensity and uptake, are still rising, which partly cushions test volume.","detail":"Q2 12-month-ending rates, 2025→2026: age 30-34 rose 94.4→96.3 (+2.0%), age 35-39 rose 54.6→55.4 (+1.5%), and age 40-44 rose 12.7→12.9 (+1.6%). The 2024→2026 gain is 93.5→96.3 at 30-34 and 54.0→55.4 at 35-39. Older-mother births carry higher screening rates and often more follow-on testing, so the mix shift supports revenue per test more than test count. The 30-34 group is the only one without a significance flag in 2026 Q2.","url":"https://data.cdc.gov/resource/76vv-a7x8.json","date":"2026 Q2","source_name":"CDC NCHS VSRR quarterly provisional estimates","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"This quantifies the offset to the shrinking NIPT denominator with the age mix."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"A rough US NIPT ceiling, using the denominator and the expert penetration range, sits well below the 2.9-3.4M the lead suggested once births fall to about 3.5M.","detail":"Births of ~3.50-3.58M (12 months to Q2'26, my estimate) times the 70-80% penetration experts cite gives about 2.45-2.86M deliveries already tested. Adding ~1M non-delivery pregnancies is the strategist's figure, not a measured one. Only a small share of those get screened, and the lead did not give that rate. With 100% of deliveries tested the ceiling is ~3.5M plus any non-delivery tests. The 70-80% is already largely reached, so growth has to come from penetration of the last 20-30%, repeat or reflex testing and added tiers, not from a growing denominator. I could not verify the penetration figure in this source.","url":"https://cdc.gov/nchs/pressroom/releases/20260409.html","date":"2026-04-09 (births) plus 2026 Q2 rates","source_name":"CDC NCHS press release and VSRR","source_type":"press","quote":"decreased by 1% from 2024","strength":"medium","why_new":"This turns the known 70-80% penetration claim into a numeric volume cap using current births."},
